{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795673110,"nonce":"xGam0rC3XD6kp5zr/NPJmRUuhpw=","title":"Let AI agents (Claude Code and similar) use Scarab through MCP: read runs, steps, logs and results, and maybe act (rerun, cancel, dispatch). Status: design only, to be discussed with the owner before any build. Not part of the 0.8.x line.","message":"The owner: \"I also want to add a secure MCP server to this, should we do as a plugin? it will\nalso benefit from the oidc work pending for the service accounts.\"\n\nGoal: let AI agents (Claude Code and similar) use Scarab through MCP: read runs, steps, logs\nand results; maybe act (rerun, cancel, dispatch).\n\n\n1. **Not in the 0.8.x line.** It is its own release (0.9.0 territory).\n2. **Agents never approve gates.** No approve-gate tool. \"If they want they can do it via\n   Claude in Chrome\", meaning a person-driven browser session with the passkey step-up.\n\n\nThe \"OIDC work pending for the service accounts\" turned out to be **outbound** workload\nidentity: Scarab issuing tokens to step Pods so AWS trusts them. It does not provide\nScarab-side service-account principals or OAuth for MCP clients, so it **does not help the\nMCP server**. That work is its own ticket, linked in a comment below.\n\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| A. A Scarab Plugin (ADR-0072) | **Fails.** It would break four ADR-0072 rules: ingress to Plugins is control-plane only with default-deny NetworkPolicy (`docs/adr/0072-plugins-a-governed-extension-point.md` ~264-268, rejected alternative \"every Plugin a server\" ~434); a Plugin never reads logs (~72-73); it acts as `plugin:\u003cname\u003e`, never as the viewer (~221), a confused deputy; writes outside a Run's Attempt are rejected (~430). Granting any of these would grant it to every third-party plugin. |\n| B. `/v1/mcp` inside scarab-server | **Survives with conditions; build later.** Same handlers, `authorize_scoped` and step-up apply. To follow the MCP authorization spec over HTTP, Scarab must act as an OAuth 2.1 authorization server: authorize and token endpoints for Scarab access tokens, discovery metadata, RFC 9728 Protected Resource Metadata and a `WWW-Authenticate` challenge, audience-bound tokens (RFC 8707), refresh rotation, and client registration (the 2025-11-25 spec prefers Client ID Metadata Documents; dynamic registration is optional and its metadata fetch is an SSRF surface). None of this exists. `crates/scarab-server/src/oauth.rs` is only a login *client*; `oidc.rs` signs per-run JWTs for clouds and its keys must not be reused for this. A `scarab_pat_` token at `/v1/mcp` would work but is not audience-bound, so it is not spec-compliant. Must run stateless across server replicas (no session affinity), not verified. |\n| C. `scarab mcp` in scarab-cli, over stdio | **Survives; build first.** Identity is the token owner, narrowed by the token's role ceiling and scope, re-derived per request (`crates/scarab-server/src/lib.rs` authenticate ~12891-12945). RBAC unchanged. The MCP spec tells stdio servers to take credentials from the environment, so there is no MCP-layer token and no passthrough question. The CLI already reads `SCARAB_TOKEN` (`crates/scarab-cli/src/main.rs` ~85). Needs no auth work. |\n| D. A separate MCP service | **Fails.** It either forwards the client's token to the REST API (the spec bans token passthrough) or holds its own powerful credential and re-implements RBAC (confused deputy). It needs token exchange (RFC 8693), which does not exist. |\n\n\nRun logs, PR titles and step results carry text that outsiders write. An agent reading them\ncan be steered into acting with its owner's token. Checked in\n`crates/scarab-server/src/step_up/ops.rs`:\n\n- `repo.dispatch` is `NotGoverned` (no step-up), so an injected agent can dispatch with\n  attacker-chosen inputs. `step.rerun`, `run.cancel` and `gate.reject` are not governed either\n  (auditor's reading, not all re-checked).\n- `gate.approve` is `Conditional`: it needs step-up only when the Run's Environment has\n  approvers. The owner's decision above removes approve from the tool set anyway.\n- For governed operations, a token gets a one-time confirmation link (ADR-0073 §6); an injected\n  agent could spam its owner with them.\n\nWhat already holds: the debug shell is browser-only; a token cannot mint tokens; a token with a\nViewer ceiling is a real read-only credential.\n\nGaps found:\n- a token has a role and a scope but no per-verb allowlist;\n- the auditor could not verify that a token's id reaches Run events, so it is unclear whether\n  audit can tell an agent's action from its owner's;\n- ADR-0082 (redacting a secret's tail at the start of a log part) is still Proposed, so a log\n  tool can return secret fragments.\n\n\n1. `scarab mcp` (stdio) in scarab-cli with read-only tools: runs, steps, logs, results. The docs\n   tell users to use a project-scoped token with a Viewer ceiling. Log output is returned as\n   untrusted data.\n2. Opt-in write tools behind a flag: rerun, cancel, reject, dispatch. Never approve.\n3. Server side: put the token id on Run events, and consider a per-token allowlist of actions.\n   The owner flagged dispatch as the open call; per-token limits should come before it.\n4. Only then a remote `/v1/mcp` (option B), with \"Scarab as an OAuth 2.1 authorization server\"\n   as its own ADR.\n\nMCP spec churn (registration and discovery changed between 2025-06-18 and 2025-11-25) then\nlands in the CLI crate, versioned against `openapi.json`, not in the server.\n\n\n1. Who uses it first: developers' Claude Code against Scarab (step 1 covers them), or hosted\n   or CI agents with no person behind them (they need Scarab service-account principals, which\n   do not exist; every token is person-owned today)?\n2. Dispatch: allowed for agents at all, and only with per-token limits?\n3. Which read tools are worth having first, and do logs need a size cap or a tail-only default?\n\n\n- `docs/adr/0072-plugins-a-governed-extension-point.md`, `docs/adr/0076-libraries-and-plugins-through-invoke.md`\n- `docs/adr/0049-identity-and-access.md`, `docs/handoffs/issued-api-tokens.md` (tokens narrow\n  their owner; no service accounts, no token endpoint)\n- `docs/adr/0073-step-up-confirmation.md`, `docs/adr/0079-scarab-authority-needs-scarab-proof.md`,\n  `docs/adr/0080-principals-have-an-internal-id.md`\n- `crates/scarab-server/src/step_up/ops.rs` (which operations are governed)\n- `crates/scarab-identity/src/lib.rs` ~419-427 (the seam for a machine-owned token)\n- MCP authorization spec, 2025-06-18 and 2025-11-25 versions","files":null}]}